[
    {
        "content": "<p>(I might have made some thread for this a few years ago, but the situation has changed so I'd like to make a new one.)</p>\n<p>We have two independent typeclasses which separately imply <code>IsBot 0</code>, these being <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=CanonicallyOrderedAdd#doc\">docs#CanonicallyOrderedAdd</a> and <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=LinearOrderedCommMonoidWithZero#doc\">docs#LinearOrderedCommMonoidWithZero</a>. There are a bunch of different convenience lemmas we can prove with this assumption, such as <code>0 &lt; x ↔ x ≠ 0</code>, <code>0 ≤ x ↔ x = 0</code>, <code>x &lt; y → 0 &lt; y</code>, etc. Annoyingly, we have some of these for one typeclass but not the other. I would like to make a new mixin <code>IsBotZeroClass</code> to collect all of these.</p>\n<p>A benefit of this is that we'd be able to generalize a bunch of theorems that are currently stated for <code>CanonicallyOrderedAdd</code>, but which truly require only the <code>IsBot 0</code> assumption (see e.g. <a href=\"https://tqft.net/mathlib4files/Mathlib/Algebra/Order/SuccPred\">file#Mathlib/Algebra/Order/SuccPred</a> for examples). This would be convenient to me, given that the types <a href=\"https://vihdzp.github.io/combinatorial-games/find/?pattern=NatOrdinal#doc\">CGT#NatOrdinal</a> and <a href=\"https://vihdzp.github.io/combinatorial-games/find/?pattern=Nimber#doc\">CGT#Nimber</a> in the CGT repo are of this form. Currently I just have a big and ugly macro to copy those theorems from <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Ordinal#doc\">docs#Ordinal</a>.</p>",
        "id": 586314552,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1776556366
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I had the impression that this class already existed! So yes, given that there are positive usecases for this class, let's add it.</p>",
        "id": 587714237,
        "sender_full_name": "Anne Baanen",
        "timestamp": 1776676323
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I'll make a PR for this soon, I'd just like to get <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/38148\">#38148</a> out of the way first.</p>",
        "id": 587714449,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1776676378
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Implementation wise, I suppose we should just go for an instance from <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=CanonicallyOrderedAdd#doc\">docs#CanonicallyOrderedAdd</a> to <code>IsBotZeroClass</code> and an <code>extends IsBotZeroClass</code> line in the definition of <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=LinearOrderedCommMonoidWithZero#doc\">docs#LinearOrderedCommMonoidWithZero</a>. There's no reasonable way to add <code>IsBotZeroClass</code> as an assumption to <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=CanonicallyOrderedAdd#doc\">docs#CanonicallyOrderedAdd</a> or <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=LinearOrderedCommMonoidWithZero#doc\">docs#LinearOrderedCommMonoidWithZero</a> (and the latter should probably be unbundled sometime soon, since deep inheritance chains are quite slow).</p>",
        "id": 587715017,
        "sender_full_name": "Anne Baanen",
        "timestamp": 1776676555
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>PR is ready: <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/38730\">#38730</a></p>",
        "id": 591865993,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1777486347
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I hope it's ok to revive this thread with a question about whether it's acceptable to duplicate lemmas that have analogues when stated both for <code>\\bot</code> and for <code>0</code> when assuming <code>IsBotZeroClass</code>. In particular, in <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/41074\">#41074</a> I add a <code>@[simp]</code> lemma for <code>WithBot  α</code> stating <code>(⊥ : α) ≤ x ↔ x ≠ ⊥</code> but this doesn't fire for <code>WithBot ENat</code> when the statement is <code>0 ≤ x</code>. Is it OK to add another <code>@[simp]</code> lemma for that case? I noticed that the lemma directly above the one I am adding already has this type of duplication, i.e. <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=WithBot.lt_coe_bot#doc\">docs#WithBot.lt_coe_bot</a> and <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=WithBot.lt_zero_iff_eq_bot#doc\">docs#WithBot.lt_zero_iff_eq_bot</a> (which could be generalized to <code>[Zero α] [Preorder α] [IsBotZeroClass α]</code> and should probably also have <code>@[simp]</code> to match)</p>\n<p>In general it may not be desirable to duplicate everything, but for <code>@[simp]</code> lemmas it seems desirable to have.</p>",
        "id": 614066792,
        "sender_full_name": "Vlad Tsyrklevich",
        "timestamp": 1785586176
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><code>WithBot ENat</code> is not <code>IsBotZeroClass</code>, so isn't this a new lemma and not a duplication? Or do you mean the inner part is <code>IsBotZeroClass</code>? Could you add a mwe for when the duplication is necessary?</p>",
        "id": 614067942,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1785587317
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Perhaps we should solve this issue first:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">⊥</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ENat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">with_implicit</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Although this works for <code>Nat</code> instead of <code>ENat</code>, yet <code>simp</code> still doesn't see that:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- #41074</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">@[</span><span class=\"n\">to_dual</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">attr</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">le_coe_top</span><span class=\"kd\">]</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">WithBot</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">coe_bot_le</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">LE</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">OrderBot</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">WithBot</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">⊥</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≠</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊥</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">cases</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;;&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">WithBot</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≠</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊥</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- does nothing even though `(⊥ : ℕ) = 0` in implicit transparency</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">with_implicit</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">exact</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">WithBot</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">coe_bot_le</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- but it does apply</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 614068810,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1785588117
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes, your mwe works where my question was whether it made to add something like</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">@[</span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"kd\">]</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">LE</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Zero</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">IsBotZeroClass</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">WithBot</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≠</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊥</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">cases</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;;&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>to close the goal in the given example.</p>",
        "id": 614071318,
        "sender_full_name": "Vlad Tsyrklevich",
        "timestamp": 1785590664
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><del>I suspect this might be hitting some special-casing for <code>Nat</code>s in the whnf alg, since this works:</del></p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MyNat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">deriving</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">LE</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">OrderBot</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">WithBot</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MyNat</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">WithBot</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">some</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">⊥</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MyNat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≠</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊥</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">WithBot</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">coe_bot_le</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p><del>though I might be biased towards that explanation since I recently learned it, so idk if it's correct</del><br>\nEdit: no that's not it<br>\n(btw the <code>WithBot.some</code> is necessary with <code>Nat</code> to avoid the cast becoming a <code>NatCast</code> or an <code>OfNat</code>, which is another issue)</p>",
        "id": 614072639,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1785592219
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes, this sort of duplication is unfortunately needed, because there are simply different ways of saying <code>WithBot.some 0</code>: as <code>0</code>, or as <code>WithBoth.some ⊥</code>. And <code>0</code> is the simp normal form for <code>WithBot NNReal</code>, but  in other cases, the <code>0</code> notation is not available.</p>",
        "id": 614200535,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1785704494
    }
]